-- title:
--   Uniqueness of ring maps from an absolutely unramified r-adic base
-- statement:
--   Fix a natural number $r$ which is prime (as a `Fact` instance). Let $\mathcal O$ be a commutative ring which is a domain of characteristic zero, assumed to be a discrete valuation ring, let $\pi \in \mathcal O$ be irreducible, assume $\mathcal O$ is adically complete for the ideal $(\pi)$, assume the quotient $\mathcal O/(\pi)$ has cardinality exactly $r$ (in the sense of `Nat.card`), and assume the absolute unramifiedness condition that the ideal generated by the image of $r$ in $\mathcal O$ equals $(\pi)$. Let $C$ be any commutative ring in which the image of $r$ is nilpotent. Then any two ring homomorphisms $f, g : \mathcal O \to C$ are equal. The conclusion is an equality of ring homomorphisms, so it asserts in particular that $\mathcal O$ admits at most one ring homomorphism to each such $C$. Of the hypotheses, the proof uses only primality of $r$, irreducibility of $\pi$, the residue cardinality, the equality $(r) = (\pi)$ and the nilpotence of $r$ in $C$.
--
--   This is the rigidity of the absolutely unramified $r$-adic base: such an $\mathcal O$ behaves like $\mathbb Z_r$, which is initial among rings in which $r$ is nilpotent. It is used in the Čerednik–Drinfeld part of the development to show that lifts along surjections are automatically $\mathcal O$-linear and unique, being cited by [`CerednikDrinfeld.FormalOmega.existsUnique_algHom_comp_eq_of_surjective_of_isNilpotent`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.FormalOmega.existsUnique_algHom_comp_eq_of_surjective_of_isNilpotent).
